TITLE:: RLTimeSpans
summary:: Time-spans and what moves them (Lewin): a context for rhythm, with rhythms as walks
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLWalk, Classes/RLTransform, Classes/RLMetre, Classes/RCSieve, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
A time-span is code::[onset, duration]::, a plain Array, the duration above zero. The spans are also the elements of a group, code::[u, d]::, with

code::
[u1, d1] [u2, d2] = [u1 + d1 u2, d1 d2]
::

which moves them from both sides, as T, I and P, L, R move triads:

table::
## on the left || code::[u, d] [t, D] = [u + d t, d D]:: || everything stretched by d and shifted by u
## on the right || code::[t, D] [u, d] = [t + D u, D d]:: || "move by u of my own durations, then scale my duration by d"
::

The two commute. A strong::rhythm is a walk::: a first span and a word of right steps (link::Classes/RLWalk::, whose "chords" are here the spans). The step from a note to the next is code::[1, r]::, r the ratio of their durations; so the same word, from another span, is the same rhythm at another time and tempo, and several voices on one word are a canon in augmentation. One step repeated is an accelerando or a ritardando.

code::
~time = RLTimeSpans.new;
~time.define(\same, [1, 1]);  ~time.define(\half, [1, 0.5]);  ~time.define(\twice, [1, 2]);
~walk = RLWalk(~time, [0, 1], [\half, \same, \twice, \same]);
~walk.chords;                                   // [0, 1], [1, 0.5], [1.5, 0.5], [2, 1], [3, 1]
~time.stretch(1.5).(~walk.chords.last);         // [4.5, 1.5]
RLWalk(~time, [2, 1.5], ~walk.word).chords;     // the same rhythm, later and slower
RLWalk(~time, [0, 1], ~time.right(1, 0.5) ! 4).chords;     // each note half as long as the one before

~time.word([1, 0.5, 0.5, 1, 1]);                // the steps of a list of durations
~time.interval([0, 1], [3, 2]);                 // [3, 2]: three of my durations later, twice as long
~time.wait([2, 0.5], 3);                        // 2: the cue at 3 is two of this span's durations away

// the 3, 3, 2 rhythm of a sieve as spans, then as a pattern
~spans = ~time.spansOf(RCSieve.residues(8, [0, 3, 6], 1/2).points(0, 8));
Pbind(#[\delta, \sustain], Pseq(~time.events(~spans)), \degree, Pseq([0, 2, 4], inf)).play;
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new

INSTANCEMETHODS::

SUBSECTION:: Transformations

METHOD:: left
The spans stretched by d and shifted by u, all alike: an link::Classes/RLTransform::.

METHOD:: right
Each span moved by u of its own durations, its duration scaled by d.

METHOD:: shift
METHOD:: stretch
code::left(u, 1):: and code::left(0, d)::.

METHOD:: define
Names a right step code::[u, d]::, or the step a word does (its steps in the order of time).

METHOD:: transform
A step by its name (or the transformation itself).

METHOD:: names
METHOD:: act
The named steps; what one makes of a span, as a list.

METHOD:: interval
METHOD:: leftInterval
The right step from one span to another (Lewin's interval), and the left one, as elements code::[u, d]::.

SUBSECTION:: Rhythms

METHOD:: spans
A list of durations as spans from an onset, each starting where the one before ends.

METHOD:: word
The right steps between the notes of a list of durations: code::[1, ratio]:: each.

METHOD:: spansOf
The spans between successive time points (the points of an link::Classes/RCSieve::, for one).

METHOD:: events
Spans as code::[delta, sustain]:: pairs, the time to the next onset and the duration: what a Pbind takes.

METHOD:: canon
A canon: one list of spans per left element code::[u, d]::, the leader's spans shifted by u and d times as slow. Every voice keeps the leader's word.
code::
~time.canon(~time.spans([1, 0.5, 0.5, 1, 1]), [[0, 1], [0, 2], [2, 0.5]]);     // the leader, twice as slow, twice as fast from 2
::

METHOD:: endTogether
The left element of a voice d times as slow (or as fast) that ends when the leader ends.

METHOD:: wait
How long until a cue, counted in the span's own durations; negative once the cue is past. The truth value of "after the cue" for time-spans.

SUBSECTION:: The group

METHOD:: identity
METHOD:: mul
METHOD:: inv
On elements code::[u, d]::; results are rounded at the tenth decimal so that equal spans compare equal.

METHOD:: element
METHOD:: chordOf
METHOD:: isCentral
METHOD:: center
METHOD:: identityTransform
METHOD:: system
What link::Classes/RLTransform:: and link::Classes/RLWalk:: ask of a context: a span is its own element.
